### Legacy draft

Players field Armies and fight battles. Armies are kept in a roster, and battles are fought on a battlefield. These activities combine to become a Campaign. 

A Campaign is a way of organizing gameplay. There are two activities that each campaign must have: an opening and a conclusion. The opening of a campaign involves determining the terms of the campaign, such as the number and composition of activities, and any objectives that players should try to achieve. This is followed by participating in those activities; in the most basic case, a campaign will have each player build an army, then fight in a battle against each other. Finally, players conclude a campaign, which involves determining the victor. More complex campaigns may add, remove, or modify activities. 

Building an army involves selecting and configuring units to fill out an army list until the player is satisfied. Armies are organized by a roster system. Rosters are lists of armies the player has made. A roster may have its own restrictions on how the army is built in order for an army to qualify for it. The default roster has no restrictions. 

Fighting a battle involves players participating in four stages: planning, deployment, engagement, and consolidation. A battle is generally fought between two opposing armies of similar army value, on a single battlefield, until one army has been wiped out. Each stage may have additional steps provided by the campaign, or rules in a player's army. Similarly, a campaign can modify how players engage in battle, such as (not comprehensive) by enforcing a turn count, including rules that affect a given stage, adding army participation restrictions, or changing victory conditions.

In the planning stage, players select an army from a roster to participate in the battle. In the deployment stage, players deploy their armies to the battlefield. In the engagement stage, players command their armies in a repeating cycle of phases called turns. In the consolidation stage, players determine the victor of the battle.

